WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.500
Don't believe the newspaper headlines that
are saying,

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:07.520
‘The rich are going to make out like bandits
with this tax legislation.’ Not true at all.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:09.760
This is going to make the tax code more progressive,

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.760
which is not a good thing from my point of
view.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:17.080
Hi, this is Nick Gillespie for Reason.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.600
Today we're talking with Chris Edwards.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.380
He's the Director of Tax Policy at the Cato
Institute

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:26.100
and we're talking about Republican tax reform
and the implications for the economy

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.260
and what it means for libertarians.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.360
Chris, thanks for talking to us.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.500
Thanks a lot for having me, Nick.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:36.880
Give us your general score card. Is this good?
Is it bad? Is it somewhere in between?

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.560
The main driver here is the corporate tax
reforms.

00:00:40.569 --> 00:00:43.659
The United States has the highest corporate
tax rate in the world.

00:00:43.659 --> 00:00:47.440
The Republicans would slash the corporate
tax rate down pretty dramatically

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:48.719
down to 20% at the federal level.

00:00:48.719 --> 00:00:49.879
And that's from 35%?

00:00:49.879 --> 00:00:53.050
From 35 down to 20%.

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:57.780
That sounds pretty low, but actually the global
average rate is only 24% today.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.960
If you think about the United States, we've
got our federal rate,

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:06.720
but we've got state corporate taxes on top
up to about 10% or more.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:12.180
Even with the Republican cuts, we'd only just
barely start getting competitive globally.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:13.620
That's the corporate stuff.

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:16.790
Before we get to the individual side, with
the corporate stuff,

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:21.000
one of the things that opponents of tax reform
say is,

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.799
‘So, corporations make a huge amount of
money. They're solelists literally and figuratively.’

00:01:25.799 --> 00:01:33.009
Why are we cutting taxes on corporations and
what is the thinking behind that?

00:01:33.009 --> 00:01:34.570
Why is that a good thing?

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:38.450
You'll hear critics say, ‘American corporations
are usually profitable today.

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:42.229
A lot of them are sitting on a big pile of
cash. Why do they need tax cuts?’

00:01:42.229 --> 00:01:45.420
The reason is because corporations are forward
looking.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:49.340
They think about building a new factory here
versus in Mexico or China,

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:51.600
and they look at that stream of future profits

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.680
and they look at how much the government tax
grab on that will be.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:00.270
They might be sitting on cash now, but the
reason they're not investing it

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:03.290
is because the U.S. corporate tax rate is
so high.

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:06.659
You cut the corporate tax rate, corporations
at the margin,

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:08.360
there would be more factories,

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:13.550
more hiring will make more sense in the United
States versus our competitors.

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:18.080
They will invest more in the long run, and
they'll build more factories.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:20.300
You build more factories, you got to hire
more workers,

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:23.370
the demand for U.S. workers will go up and
then wages will rise.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:24.410
This sounds like Trump's, but this is Trump's
nightmare,

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:28.250
because then suddenly people from Mexico are
going to start coming back to the United States

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:30.190
instead of leaving, which is what's happening.

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:32.620
You know the demand for labor will go up a
lot.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:36.790
The reality, Nick, is that even though the
U.S. unemployment rate is pretty low now,

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:40.030
our participation rate in the labor force
has been falling,

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:43.830
especially for middle-aged men for a number
of decades now.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:47.210
We want to get a lot of those folks off the
sidelines back in the workforce.

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:50.650
I think the corporate tax cut really goes
to that issue.

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:55.460
There's also a shift, and I guess this affects
both the corporate as well as the individual

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:56.460
rates.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:00.470
The U.S. currently taxes on a global basis.

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:02.770
Both plans would shift into a territorial
basis.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:05.020
What's the difference and why is that important?

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:06.260
Right now, U.S. corporations,

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:10.020
you think about a big corporation like General
Motors or DuPont or Intel,

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:13.530
they have subsidiaries in dozens and dozens
of countries around the world.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:18.910
The United States today says, ‘We're going
to tax you on your global operations.’

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:19.910
Think about this.

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:22.000
DuPont, let's say they have a subsidiary in
Brazil

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.990
making some chemical product to supply the
Brazilian market.

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:29.800
The U.S. government taxes those profits right
now.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:32.560
But you think about a German chemical company

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.330
with a subsidiary in Brazil serving the Brazilian
market,

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:39.350
the German government at home doesn't really
care what the subsidiary does,

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:42.830
and so it's more competitive than the U.S.
subsidiary.

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:44.510
How does that affect us?

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:47.660
We want our foreign subsidiaries to be competitive

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.960
because they expand their sales and global
markets.

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:54.630
The profits pour back into the United States
and that tends to suck exports out of the

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:55.630
United States.

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:57.920
When our corporations do well abroad,

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:04.250
they take a lot of imported, intermediate
goods from their U.S. production.

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:10.180
We want our multinationals to do well, it
means they'll invest here,

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:13.980
more global headquarters of companies will
locate here,

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:18.760
and their foreign subsidiaries will do well,
which is good for our workers in our economy.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:22.210
As you know, there's been this drain over
the last few decades

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:25.780
on multinationals moving their headquarters
out of the United States,

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:30.300
because it's a punishing place to locate the
headquarters of multinationals.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.180
Republicans moved to what's called a territorial
system,

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:40.150
so that'll draw multinational headquarters
back to the United States.

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:43.910
The corporate side, or the business side of
this seems pretty good,

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:47.320
and from a libertarian, from a free market
angle, there's not that much to complain about

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:50.600
other than rates could always be lower.

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:53.020
The individual side is more of a shit show,
right?

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:59.690
Absolutely. It's basically reassembling deck
chairs on a really messy and horribly complex

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:00.690
system.

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:02.990
There is a little bit of simplification.

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:04.600
Republicans double the standard deduction,

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:07.760
so that means more people will be taking the
standard deduction,

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:11.540
less people taking itemized deductions like
the mortgage interest deduction.

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:13.080
That's good. It's a bit of a simplification.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:15.440
There's a little bit of rate cut in.

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:19.630
I think that the joint committee on taxation,
the official scoring body,

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:23.400
found that there would be a better average
of about a two percent marginal rate cut on

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:24.590
the individual side,

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:25.600
sort of on average.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:31.590
That's good, but there will be a lot of new
complexity,

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:36.730
Republicans will cut the tax rate on so-called
pass-through or small businesses a little

00:05:36.730 --> 00:05:37.730
bit,

00:05:37.730 --> 00:05:40.060
but the rules will be very complicated.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:42.340
If you're a law firm, you don't get the rate
cut,

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:45.150
but if you're a small manufacturing firm,
you do get the rate cut,

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:49.590
there will be a lot of lobbying and fighting
over those sorts of breaks.

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:54.630
Why is it good to get people out of itemizing
deductions?

00:05:54.630 --> 00:06:00.060
You said by increasing a standard deduction.
Why is itemization bad?

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:03.230
I think it treats people unfairly.

00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:06.710
Right now, renters don't get the special break
from the government,

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:10.110
but homeowners do get the special break from
the government.

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:12.740
It's the same as other itemized deductions.

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:14.920
Why should the government be playing favorites,

00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:17.340
giving special breaks for some Americans and
not others?

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:20.720
I think we ought to have low rates and equal
treatment across the board.

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:23.550
You pay a certain flat percentage on what
you earn, although,

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:26.620
I would have a large deduction at the bottom,

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:30.100
because there's no point taxing people who
are below the poverty level,

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:32.150
because they get the government benefits anyways,

00:06:32.150 --> 00:06:37.190
so have a big giant exemption and then tax
everyone a flat rate across the board equally.

00:06:37.190 --> 00:06:40.870
We're in a situation where there might even
be a majority of Americans

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:45.919
living in households that pay no federal income
tax.

00:06:45.919 --> 00:06:47.740
On a certain level, that seems great.

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:49.400
You're poor, you shouldn't be paying it.

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:51.860
When you're talking about half of the population,

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:57.610
why does it present a problem to take everybody
off the tax rolls?

00:06:57.610 --> 00:07:01.480
One of the reasons why you want to go to a
flat tax where everyone pays an equal amount

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:04.460
is then everyone feels the pain of big government.

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:07.889
Taxes are price of government, you want people
to feel the price,

00:07:07.889 --> 00:07:12.520
and so that if they feel the full price of
government it limits the demand for big government.

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:13.520
That's a good thing.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:14.520
You're right.

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:19.699
About 45% of U.S. households now pay absolutely
no federal income tax unfortunately.

00:07:19.699 --> 00:07:23.330
The tax code is extremely progressive, to
use the liberal word for that.

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:29.139
I use the word graduated, meaning people at
the top pay this huge bulk of taxes.

00:07:29.139 --> 00:07:30.830
As you know, some of these numbers,

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:35.730
the top one percent of highest earners pay
40% of all federal income taxes.

00:07:35.730 --> 00:07:36.949
It's really incredible,

00:07:36.949 --> 00:07:41.249
and that to share the burden paid by the top
group has gone up and up and up,

00:07:41.249 --> 00:07:43.360
which means that the share paid by everyone
else,

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:48.050
by most voters has gone down, so they're demanding
more government.

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:49.479
That's not a good thing.

00:07:49.479 --> 00:07:51.020
Here's a startling statistic.

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:55.260
The United States has the most progressive
or graduated income tax code

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:57.699
of all the OECD industrial countries.

00:07:57.699 --> 00:08:00.419
The most progressive of all. It's remarkable,
and this is America.

00:08:00.419 --> 00:08:06.050
That means that it goes from relatively poor
people pay little or no taxes

00:08:06.050 --> 00:08:10.100
and the wealthier you are, the more highly
taxes you are.

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:12.360
Don't believe the newspaper headlines that
are saying,

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:16.979
‘The rich are going to make out like bandits
with this tax legislation.’ Not true at

00:08:16.979 --> 00:08:17.979
all.

00:08:17.979 --> 00:08:19.620
This is going to make the tax code more progressive,

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:21.850
which is not a good thing from my point of
view.

00:08:21.850 --> 00:08:26.449
The other thing is that the overall level
of revenue that's generated ...

00:08:26.449 --> 00:08:32.479
For most of 21st century, we've been pulling
in government revenue from all sources,

00:08:32.479 --> 00:08:35.669
about 70% of GDP, right around there.

00:08:35.669 --> 00:08:38.669
There were some declines during the recession
and whatnot.

00:08:38.669 --> 00:08:42.729
Will this tax reform increase that amount,
and if it does,

00:08:42.729 --> 00:08:45.980
is that a good thing from a libertarian market
point of view?

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:50.930
Do we want the government to be actually taking
more income out of our pockets?

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:56.110
The Republicans have set aside a trillion
and a half dollars over 10 years for this

00:08:56.110 --> 00:08:57.600
tax cut,

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:01.730
and that's scored in static basis.

00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:04.620
Some of the business reforms will increase.

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:09.990
GDP will move the economy upwards, so that'll
cause a reflow of revenue to the government.

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:12.749
However, it's still going to be a revenue
loser for the government.

00:09:12.749 --> 00:09:17.339
This will be a net tax cut overall. The reason
is, as we were just discussing, a lot of the

00:09:17.339 --> 00:09:21.990
individual tax changes don't do anything for
economic growth, so they're just a pure revenue

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:27.690
loss to the government. Those sorts of tax
cuts, I could take it or leave it. If you

00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:32.851
believe in starve the beast, then yeah, let
people keep more of their money. For me, I

00:09:32.851 --> 00:09:37.329
think if we're going to cut taxes, we ought
to cut taxes in a way that makes the overall

00:09:37.329 --> 00:09:41.160
economy grow more strongly.
Let's talk a little bit about starve the beast,

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:45.920
which is the idea that if you reduce the amount
of money that government can raise it will

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:53.059
spend less. That pretty clearly is a wrong
theory because we've been borrowing and borrowing

00:09:53.059 --> 00:09:58.470
more and more money, and nobody did that more
than George W. Bush, and Barack Obama followed

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:04.629
in his footsteps of putting more and more
on a national credit card. Is there any connection,

00:10:04.629 --> 00:10:09.679
because this is what Republican proponents
of tax reform say, ‘We're going to get the

00:10:09.679 --> 00:10:12.639
government, we're going to drop taxes and
the drag in the economy, and then we're going

00:10:12.639 --> 00:10:18.129
to get spend in cuts.’ Is there any reason
to believe that, or what are the steps that

00:10:18.129 --> 00:10:22.470
lead from tax reform to spending cuts down
the road?

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:25.720
Let me start at the state government level.
State governments are required to balance

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:30.860
their budgets every year. Starve the beast
does probably work, because a governor cuts

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:36.130
taxes, then it really forces the legislature
down the road to cut the spending. U.S. federal

00:10:36.130 --> 00:10:40.800
government, of course, can spend as much as
it wants, so starve the beast doesn't seem

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:45.800
to work anymore. I think a few decades back,
starve the beast did work because politicians

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:50.490
were scared of big deficits. You saw during
the '80s, Reagan cuts taxes and then there

00:10:50.490 --> 00:10:55.160
was a big movement with various budget reform
plans to try to restrain spending. That is

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:59.431
no longer the case, and the reason is because
we have global capital markets. The U.S. government

00:10:59.431 --> 00:11:04.100
can borrow as much as it wants and it doesn't
push up interest rates. It doesn't push up

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:07.509
people mortgage rates. It doesn't cause any
pain for politicians.

00:11:07.509 --> 00:11:12.899
Why should we worry about deficits? This is
something, when Republicans are in power and

00:11:12.899 --> 00:11:17.129
they're running up deficits, Democrats are
like, ‘We need fiscal sanity.’ When Democrats

00:11:17.129 --> 00:11:21.480
are in power, the Republicans suddenly become
deficit hawks and then that disappears very

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:27.510
quickly. Why is mounting national debt a problem
if the U.S. can keep borrowing money essentially

00:11:27.510 --> 00:11:30.579
for free?
A couple reasons. One is most economists think

00:11:30.579 --> 00:11:34.619
that some big financial or economic crash
will come down the road as our debt keeps

00:11:34.619 --> 00:11:40.379
piling up over the decades. The second reason
is just the ethics of it. Borrowing now deficit's

00:11:40.379 --> 00:11:45.670
debt, pushes costs to the future. You can
see that from a libertarian perspective very

00:11:45.670 --> 00:11:49.310
simply. Right now, if the government has a
big spending program, it goes out and borrows

00:11:49.310 --> 00:11:53.960
10 billion dollars for a spending program,
no one is hurt in the short run. People will

00:11:53.960 --> 00:11:57.889
get the spending benefit, the creditors will
lend more money to the government. They don't

00:11:57.889 --> 00:12:02.699
mind because they're doing it voluntarily,
so no one seems to lose. No one does now.

00:12:02.699 --> 00:12:06.740
In the future, we got to pay back the debt,
the government's got to go out and use its

00:12:06.740 --> 00:12:11.550
coercion to raise the taxes. From a libertarian
perspective, you can see the pain is moved

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:21.300
to the future when the government borrows
more now, and that's unethical, I think.

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:26.139
Is there some event horizon where we're actually
going to cut year over year spending, or is

00:12:26.139 --> 00:12:34.339
this just kind of smoke and mirrors? Back
in the early '90s, George H. W. Bush pushed

00:12:34.339 --> 00:12:39.480
through a tax plan, which was wildly unpopular.
He had made a ‘no new taxes’ pledge. All

00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:44.720
the Republicans, all conservatives, all libertarians
said he was a sucker because he made the rookie

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:50.139
mistake of raising taxes now in the name of
spending cuts down the road and the spending

00:12:50.139 --> 00:12:55.420
cuts never come. Now we're in a world where
we're doing tax cuts with spending cuts promised.

00:12:55.420 --> 00:13:01.639
Are we just these big suckers?
Perhaps there's no relationship between taxes

00:13:01.639 --> 00:13:06.040
and spending at all. At the federal level,
politicians like to cut taxes, they like to

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:09.850
increase spending. Maybe there's no relationship.
I'll give you one way that the Republican

00:13:09.850 --> 00:13:14.199
tax cuts, if they go through in the next couple
weeks may be positive. That is, it does take

00:13:14.199 --> 00:13:18.899
the whole tax reform issue off the plate of
the Republican politicians for a while. For

00:13:18.899 --> 00:13:25.949
many years, Republican politicians, they can
prove their conservative bonafides by claiming

00:13:25.949 --> 00:13:30.449
they're for big tax reform, and it's always
a crowd pleaser with Republican voters.

00:13:30.449 --> 00:13:33.389
But they don't actually have to do it?
You're right. They keep saying, ‘We're going

00:13:33.389 --> 00:13:37.880
to do tax reform down the road.’ This sort
of does take it off the agenda, I think, for

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:43.809
a while. A lot of this is going to depend
on what the Trump administration does. Trump

00:13:43.809 --> 00:13:48.709
has a very good budget director, as you know,
who is pushing spending cuts. I think Republicans

00:13:48.709 --> 00:13:56.120
put a tax cut in the law before Christmas
this year, then the next year it does open

00:13:56.120 --> 00:14:00.680
up the spending cutters on Capitol Hill to
really push more for spending cuts. I'll give

00:14:00.680 --> 00:14:04.989
you an example of that. The House Freedom
Caucus now, the most conservative members

00:14:04.989 --> 00:14:10.939
in the House, they're holding their fire now
on spending cuts because they want the tax

00:14:10.939 --> 00:14:15.180
cut to go through first. The tax cut goes
through, sign in the law, I think it's going

00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:21.209
to really open up the scenarios for spending
cuts.

00:14:21.209 --> 00:14:24.489
To make meaningful spending cuts, you got
to go where the money is, and that really

00:14:24.489 --> 00:14:29.790
means old age entitlements, Medicare, and
social security, and defense spending. None

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:36.899
of those seem to be anything that Republicans
are known for even pretending that they want

00:14:36.899 --> 00:14:42.209
to cut. Will those be on the table, assuming
tax reform goes through?

00:14:42.209 --> 00:14:47.489
To Trump's credit, he actually does go after
some of the entitlement. Social security disability

00:14:47.489 --> 00:14:53.310
insurance is an 150 dollar billion program.
It is a giant spending program and it's hugely

